Subject: PPL: Many Types of Intellectual Property Are Not Protected

At the risk of repeating things that may have been said earlier, (I'm new to
the list) what is the real difference between a patent and a title?  Both are
pieces of paper that represent a claim on something.  In one case, the claim 
is on something that is three dimensional and in the other it is not, but in
both cases the title is still an abstract concept, and the title is what will
be presented in "court" as evidence of ownership.
	You may argue that it is easier to defend land than an intellectual
idea, but that is only in the case that you have to _defend_ it--meaning 
actually engaging in a battle with someone.  However, if it ever came down to
that, the side with the bigger guns would undoubtedly win (at least in the
short run).  In general the battle over land ownership would take place in the
abstract in an arbitration agency, and would use things like titles to 
demonstrate evidence of ownership.
	Title insurance companies much like those we have today would spring up
and offer insurance to those people wanting to protect their ownership of an
idea.  There is nothing here that necessitates the use of coercive force or a
political state.
	Anyway, I hope I haven't said a bunch of stuff that others have already
brought up.

