Date: Mon, 1 Feb 1993 23:25:55 -0500 (EST)
From: esr@snark.thyrsus.com (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: Outlawry

> Eric writes: "...someone who done my client wrong does a bunk to La-La land."
> and: "...declares someone a wolf's-head, ..."
> 
> huh?

Sorry.

The first reference is heavily slangy; read "someone who injured my client
flees to Los Angeles to avoid prostitution".

In the Icelandic sagas, "wolf's-head" was a synonym for "outlaw".  A court
might declare a man "wolf's-head", meaning the normal penalties for murdering
him no longer applied (in particular, his kin would not be able to collect
weregild from the slayer).

Outlawry was rare, and usually declared only as a penalty for murder with
aggravating circumstances.  Icelandic law made some interesting distinctions
in this regard; for example, "night-murder" (by stealth or ambush) was regarded
as a much more serious crime than a public slaying.  Also, murder in violation
of guest custom (that is, of host by guest or vice versa) was considered a
particularly heinous crime.  Either of these was a fast route to outlawry.

Icelandic law is fairly well documented in the sagas and makes an instructive
study for anarchists.  The Icelanders maintained a working anarchy with PPL
for nearly five hundred years.  Some of its lessons are still very relevant.
-- 
					Eric S. Raymond <esr@snark.thyrsus.com>

