in Germany... I must have adapted to the German culture better than
I thought :)

} I believe that there are people who pursue power (control over other
} people). Socialism is "their system". Under socialism those who
} maximize power win.

I must defer to David Cake, the same can be said for capitalists pursuing
economic power.  In most countries, the mixed economy is "their system".
Under any system, those who maximize power win.

} The idea that the poor are the ones who revolt is completely unfounded
} and not based in any facts, revolutions are always the work of
} intellectuals, and so is socialism, poor folks are generally 
} conservative and extremely money oriented.

David has already countered that many of the poor and conservative 
are indeed socialist, in the since of wanting redistribution.  I 
would go further and state that the poor are the ones who revolt.
It is very common for large numbers of the masses to be killed,
whereas intellectuals have a habit of escaping for the most part.
Even in China, the numbers of students who died were larger than
the number of their leaders.  The intellectuals incite others to
revolt... 

Also, there is a biased habit of calling anyone who starts a revolution
an intellectual.  This habit doesn't recognize the guy who shoots all
his co-workers at work as a revolutionary, or Oswald for that matter.
Or better yet, an uneducated carpenter's son... who started up a major
religion.

With regards to totalitarianism under the pure form of socialism... 
that isn't my point.  I am not arguing in the slightest for a socialist
state, I am on the other hand trying to figure out why we have socialism
in our mixed economies.  It is exactly because I believe that socialism
is the tool of capitalists that I do not fear that it will go unchecked
by opposition.  The oppositionist to total socialism, are the ones who
should be influencing the use of redistribution in the first place.

} Socialism may start out as capitalists attempts to buy out radicals.

I have never heard of such a situation.  Socialism started out in theory
by decadent philosophers, and was put into practice by extreme political
intellectuals.  The Morgans and Carnegie's of the time were hardly the
type to practice socialism.

But that does bring up an interesting thought.  Socialism as I would 
have it is the redistribution of "minimal opportunity" ie. power.
Technology has changed society, and in the change... socialism.
In reference to Alvin Toffler's Powershift, at first, power was
concentrated on land ownership and the agriculture.  The tools of power
were limited... violence was the only tool.  This fits in neatly with
the original term socialism with regards to property rights.  Those
who fought against socialism were the land owners who didn't wish
to give up power ie. property rights.  Revolt was put down violently,
or land was given up.

But, as technology grew, and we moved toward the industrial age power
shifted from the land to the industry... the focus of power from 
violence to money.  Money was more fluid and could be used to reward
as well as punish.  Those who were prone to revolt and wanted "minimal
opportunity" instead of asking for a plot of land, asked for higher "minimum"
wages.  This was the new socialism.  Those who held large sums of money,
the Morgan's and Carnegie's didn't want to give up power ie money.  So
they put down with the tool at hand, violence or they lost and made
monetary concessions.

Today, we are moving towards the service industry/information age.  
Information is more valuable than land or money.  With the right knowledge
you can quickly get either... without knowledge, land and money are harder
to hold on to.  Power is shifting from money to knowledge ownership.  
And socialism is shifting accordingly.  People today are increasingly asking
for retraining and educational opportunities... and welfare reforms are
allowed to reduce monetary concessions in return for knowledge concessions.
capital is increasingly cheaper with regards to knowledge... the tools
of power are violence and money... and the concession, knowledge.

If this is true, then calling the redistribution of land and money 
socialism is outdated... and only touching the tip of the iceberg.  Money
and property rights are increasingly cheaper... knowledge is increasingly
more valuable.  The new socialism will revolve around redistributing
information rights.  The notable new element in this all is that unlike
money and land, when we redistribute them... the old owner loses them.
With knowledge this is not true... knowledge can be recycled infinitly.
The new emphasis, as it is already evident, will be on information rights,
in particular trade secrets.  The new socialism will redistribute 
information at the cost of secrecy and privacy.

Furthermore, this new socialism is unavoidable and necessary, because
people will demand "minimal opportunity" and minimal opportunity in
the future will mean having a bit of information power.

Now for the application of "socialism" devoid of government interference!
Sounds like a paradox... but I can foresee and will bet on the emergence
of private corporations that will take in the under- and un-employed, give
them subsistence food and a roof, and retrain and reeducate the people in
return for a profitable cut of their wages for a period once they find
better employment.  What a great system!  If the people are not able to
find a better job, then the private company will be unable to collect...
so the company that does it's job makes a profit... the one that doesn't
isn't competitive and drops out.

Guess I started running away in my own thoughts... comments?

