In an e-mail message Tim Freeman <tsf@EDU.CMU.CS> writes

>I'd be more interested in predictions of what will happen under what
>conditions than in statements about what should happen.  Assume we
>will try to continue with the present scheme until it obviously fails;
>how will it fail?  Perhaps replying to sci.nanotech instead of to me
>personally would be useful.  Feel free to quote me.  I suspect Josh
>will re-route the conversation to another newsgroup.

Please don't, Josh I think all involved in this business should think
about these things. 

Why do we have mass unemployment?

     For  the  first  two  thirds  of  this  century  manufacturing  was 
dominated  by the "Taylorite" school.  Huge numbers of men were used  as 
if  they  were industrial robots.  The sign on the  workshop  door  said 
"leave  your mind outside,  we only want your body." This period saw  an 
enormous  expansion  of affluence,  both  horizontally  and  vertically. 
There  was a plentiful supply of people going from "have not" to  "have" 
to buy the products of the new efficient production.

     But  it  seems  Taylorism  has  had  it's  day.   The   competitive 
businesses  are  those  that  have  replaced  trained  but   essentially 
uneducated  workers with real robots.  Increasingly people are asked  to 
bring  their  minds to work with them.  Taylorism worked best  with  the 
majority  of people only slightly educated and a minority educated to  a 
high  level.  Both in the States and here in the UK we see  conservative 
forces  impeding  the transformation of the educational system  to  cope 
with the new realities.

     But,  it seems to me,  there is a more basic problem.  There are no 
longer large numbers of people moving from "have not" to "have",  indeed 
the movement is now almost exclusively in the other direction.  When  it 
comes  to consumer durables we are getting into a situation where  there 
is little the "haves" need, and little the "have nots" can afford.

     Of course the situation is not absolute.  There are new  households 
being set up all the time.  People tend to get richer as they get older. 
Advertising can persuade people to buy things they don't actually  need. 
What  I believe is happening,  however,  is that the inexorable rise  in 
production  efficiency  is outpacing the rise  in  consumption.  Result: 
rising unemployment.

     So  what  about nanotechnology?  Nanotechnology seems to  have  the 
potential  to raise production effeciency beyond the vaults  of  heaven. 
If  we  continue  with our present social structures  the  result  seems 
inevitable.  Mass  employment  becomes majority  unemployment  and  soon 
approaches  universal  unemployment  (especially  if  nanotechnology  is 
accompanied by major advances in AI).  Only the two oldest  professions, 
priest and prostitute, seem at all secure.

     Only one kind of policy seems likely to cushion this transition: An 
enforced  reduction in working hours without corresponding  decrease  in 
pay  to  offset the rise in efficiency.  The trouble is that  a  country 
which took this route would be at a competitive disadvantage.  It  would 
have  to be agreed at a supranational  level.  This  is,  indeed,  being 
attempted  in the EEC but,  unless trade barriers are  increased  rather 
than  removed,  the EEC doesn't seem a wide enough  unit.  (Britain  has 
refused  to accept this agreement but then we're behind most  of  Europe 
in the transition from the Taylorite era).

     As  unemployment  rises the unemployed will increase  in  political 
power and force a massive increase in their real incomes.  It's  obvious 
were  the  goods come from to satisfy these people but  where  does  the 
money  come  from?  The answer is inevitable.  One way or the  other  it 
comes from the people who had it.  The capitalists and the brain workers 
who   still  have  jobs.   This  happens  either  through  taxation   or 
hyperinflation.   This   is  likely  to  prove  a  violent   transition, 
especially, I think, in the USA.

     So what are we left with?  A society where working makes little  or 
no difference to your affluence.  A society where artifacts are seen  as 
having  little  or  no  value.  But there is  still  one  resource  that 
technology  will  do little to increase,  land.  I see wealth  and  land 
ownership  becoming synonymous (as was the case centuries ago).  Are  we 
heading  for a society where wealth is only inherited or taken by  force 
in war, never earned? Will more aspects of feudalism return as well?

     One   technology   seems  capable   of   increasing   land.   Space 
colonisation.  However history suggests that voluntary colonisation does 
little to reduce the population of the country (or in this case  planet) 
of origin.

Malcolm McMahon

